ise the up face 180 degrees both  right faces  counterclockwise  the up face 180 degrees both right faces  clockwise the up face 180 degrees the  front face 180 degrees both right faces  clockwise the front face 180 degrees  both left faces counterclockwise the  back face 180 degrees and both right  faces 180 degrees and as you'll see that  parody error which we had that piece  flipped is now corrected so now all of  the treasures are complete and we can  solve this cube exactly like a 3x3 so  now let's go over the next type of  parity error you will see another common  parity error you'll see is one way you  have to outer edge pieces which have  switched places  this outer red white piece needs to come  over here to complete the red white  tread  while this outer yellow blue piece needs  to go over there to complete the yellow  blue tread in order to fix this parity  error you must first make sure that  these two single outer edge pieces are  on the same layer if you have them on  opposite or different layers like that  you need to first arrange them so that  they are in the same layer before  continuing once they are on the same  layer we want to position them on the  top face all the way to the right of the  tread pieces that they're on so we want  it to look just like that we're then  going to perform the following algorithm  in order to correct the parity error so  we want to turn both left faces  counterclockwise the up face 180 degrees  both left faces counterclockwise the up  face 180 degrees the front face 180  degrees both left faces counterclockwise  the front face  a hundred and eighty degrees both right  faces clockwise the top face a hundred  and eighty degrees both right faces  counterclockwise the up face 180 degrees  and both left faces 180 degrees and as  you'll see that parody error in which  these pieces had been swapped that's now  corrected and this cube to me solved  exactly like a three by three the last  type of parity error you'll see is one  where we basically have the previous two  parity arrows together or even the same  parity error two or more times making  what looks like a randomized or  sometimes what's referred to as a  checkerboard pattern this type of parity  error is less common but it's a little  bit more involved however it can be  corrected using only one algorithm the  one which I used for the previous parody  case to swap those two outer edge pieces  so we don't have to use that really long  algorithm which I used in the first case  to flip that one middle edge piece over  however you can use it if you want to  the parody case I have on the left here  is a little easier since it already has  one edge pair while the parody case on  the right here is a little trickier  since it has no edge pieces lined up at  all what we always want to do is to get  the edge pieces so that they are paired  up with one another by pairing the edge  pieces we will eventually arrange them  so that they look like the previous  parody error since the parity error on  the left is a little easier I'm going to  go over that one first okay so as you'll  see we already have an edge pair here  for the red blue so we want to try to  get an edge pair for the yellow blue to  do that we need to match these two  pieces but we can't do that when they  have the same orientation to switch the  orientation I'm going to bring this  piece down put that piece over there and  then bring this piece back up now that  they have opposite orientation we can  match them up to match them up I'm going  to switch these two outer edge pieces by  using the algorithm we use in the  previous parody case  so we need to put these outer edge  pieces on the top face all the way to  the right now we can perform the  following algorithm both left faces  counterclockwise the up face 180 degrees  both left faces counterclockwise the up  face again 180 degrees the front face  180 degrees both left faces  counterclockwise the front face 180  degrees 